comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T23:47:26.217Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: the property suite now generates randomized 2-6 branch append sets and proves deterministic item convergence under original and reversed merge order across 250 runs, alongside history reanchor/replay properties. The suite found and bounded serializer-level tag whitespace normalization rather than weakening the convergence contract. Full coverage is exact 100/100/100/100."
  "2026-07-26T23:49:55.292Z","harness:codex","Final local verification: linked PM tests pass; full sandboxed suite passes 376 files and 6,437 tests at exact 100% statements (44,398/44,398), branches (31,515/31,515), functions (9,082/9,082), and lines (43,135/43,135). Lint, zero-duplicate scan, 100% docstring/static quality, SDK surface/import/transport ratchets, docs/skills, secret scan, npm audit, compatibility, packed npx, 88-command package-first dogfood, and Sentry 14-day zero-critical/zero-high gate pass. Live tracker strict validation checked 2,117 items and 2,128 history streams with zero drift, duplicate IDs, config errors, driver drift, or pending receipts. Telemetry successfully flushed to the configured endpoint at 2026-07-26T23:49:30.656Z."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T20:41:56.275Z","harness:claude-code","First live end-to-end run of this suite's core scenario against the shipped 2026.7.25 build, so the zero-conflict acceptance bar has a measured starting point. Throwaway git repository, pm merge install applied, base commit with a two-entry stream, then two branches each adding three entries to the same item, then git merge.\n\nProperties that already hold — record these as the baseline the suite must keep, not as work:\n- Zero conflict markers anywhere under .agents/pm. The only conflicts git reported were in my sandbox's PM_GLOBAL_PATH telemetry files, which I had placed inside the repository; no tracked pm artifact conflicted.\n- History union is complete and loses no author: 2 shared + 3 + 3 = 8 entries, ops create update comment_add update note_add comment_add update learning_add.\n- Field-aware convergence keeps both branches' non-contradictory edits: status in_progress from branch A and priority 1 from branch B in the same merged item.\n- Every annotation class survives from both sides: 2 comments, 1 note, 1 learning.\n- Point-in-time addressing of pre-divergence versions is stable across the merge: pm get --at 2 returns the identical state before and after.\n\nThe property that does not hold is the verifying chain after reanchor. Post-merge, pm history <id> --verify returns ok false with verify_failed:current_item_hash_mismatch (latest_after_hash f0dfb167..., current_item_hash 917a2941..., current_matches_latest false); pm validate reports validate_history_drift_hash_mismatches:1 with history_drift warn and overall ok true; and pm history-repair --dry-run reports chain_drift_before false with entries_rehashed 0, correctly declining because the chain itself verifies. The gap is that no entry describes the merged field set, so there is nothing to re-anchor to. The write-side primitive is filed as pm-9j2r3b; this suite's fourth acceptance clause is the property that proves it."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T23:47:27.022Z","harness:codex","Multi-branch conformance should generate canonical domain values and vary branch count, order, and append ownership. Serializer normalization belongs in the generator boundary; merge-order differences must never change the canonical result."
